  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1134 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nCloud cover returns today and will clear out Saturday afternoon.\nTemperatures are expected to gradually warm up to the mid 60s today \nwith some areas in the brush country potentially reaching up to 70.\nSaturday morning expect localized foggy conditions within the \ncoastal plains and into the Brush Country. Temperatures across the \narea will be in the upper 70s with winds picking up from the south \nduring the afternoon and evening. Saturday night into Sunday morning \nahead of the cold front, a surface high moving across the Gulf will \npromote southerly winds across the waters. Shelf and bay water \ntemperatures in the northern bays have cooled due to the recent \nfronts passing through. This combination of southerly flow and \ncooler shelf waters raises the probability of sea fog developing in \nthese northern bays. REFS members are hinting at the possibility \nthat sea fog could develop in Corpus Christi bay and along the \nLaguna Madre, but that would depend on if the winds continue from \nthe south or shift from the southwest.\n\nThe cold front looks to move through mid morning Sunday shifting \nwinds from the north, temperatures are only expected to drop \nslightly. Lowering temperatures overnight to the mid 40s across the \nregion, before a warming trend continues through next week reaching \ninto the 80s by mid-week. \n\nRain chances stay low, inhibited by the lingering dry air and frequent fronts \nmoving through. PWAT values stay below climate normals for this time of year, \nalso keeping the rain chances to a minimum.",
